    "product_id": "2ce4ea75-da5d-4540-88f2-434c5b6f79da",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 060849\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1249 AM PST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Breezy northerly winds are expected today and Saturday, with\n   the strongest winds with elevated wave heights in the Colorado\n   River Valley. Otherwise, quiet weather with near normal\n   temperatures are expected through the week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Thursday.\n\nA sharp upper level trough will continue to shift east today. This \nwill put the region in an area of increasing northerly flow, which \nwill result in dry conditions and breezy north winds today and \nSaturday. The pressure gradient wont be overly impressive and \nwidespread gusts 20-30 MPH gusts with stronger gusts due to terrain \nenhancement in the Colorado River Valley are expected. While \nLaughlin/Bullhead City may briefly see 40 MPH winds, northerly gusts \nin the Colorado River should remain around 30-35 MPH . Impacts to \nboating due to winds and building waves are expected on Lake Mohave \nand Lake Havasu and a Lake Wind Advisory is in effect through \nSaturday afternoon. By Monday, the trough will be lifting out of the \narea and winds will diminish with dry conditions expected through \nthe week. The coolest day should be today as high temperatures run a \ncouple degrees below normal, but as ridging builds in behind the \nexiting system, temperatures will warm through next week to near \nor slightly above normal. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light\nwesterly-to-northwesterly winds will continue through the \novernight hours. Winds will pick up and shift to the north after \nsunrise on Friday. 10 to 15 knot sustained speeds with 15 to 25 \nknot northerly wind gusts will continue into the early evening \nhours with winds becoming more northeasterly in the afternoon. \nWinds will decrease and shift to the northwest on Friday evening. \nVFR conditions will persist through the TAF period.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...For the most part, \nwinds will favor typical drainage directions with sustained speeds\nof 10 knots or less through early Friday morning. North winds \nwill pick up early Friday morning down the Colorado River Valley, \nwith peak gusts around 25 to 35 knots in the afternoon as winds \nfunnel through the terrain. Winds across the rest of the area will\nalso swing around to a more northerly direction on Friday \nmorning. However, wind speeds will not be as strong with peak \ngusts in the 20 to 25 knot range on Friday. Winds will decrease \non Friday evening with elevated northerly winds lingering down the\nColorado River Valley through the overnight period. VFR \nconditions will persist through the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
